{"name":"Metallic Green","hex":"#24855b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"metallic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, or characteristic of metal.","Made of or containing metal."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A metallic color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/metallic"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Metallic color","blocks":[{"text":"A metallic color is a color that appears to be that of a polished metal. The visual sensation usually associated with metals is its metallic shine. This cannot be reproduced by a simple solid color, because the shiny effect is due to the material's brightness varying with the surface angle to the light source. In addition, there is no mechanism for showing metallic or fluorescent colors on a computer without resorting to rendering software which simulates the action of light on a shiny surface. Consequently in art and in heraldry one would normally use a metallic paint that glitters like a real metal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metallic_color","wikidata":"Q25312687"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Platinum_crystals.jpg/500px-Platinum_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Platinum_crystals.jpg/1280px-Platinum_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Platinum_crystals.jpg/500px-Platinum_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Platinum crystals","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlatinum_crystals.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg/500px-Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg/1280px-Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg/500px-Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The damselfly Austrolestes cingulatus (the metallic ringtail) is noted for its naturally occurring metallic color. Additionally, such insect coloring often leads to circular polarization of reflected light.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAustrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg/120px-BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg/250px-BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg/120px-BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_(7241672566).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg/120px-GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg/250px-GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg/120px-GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGOLD_META